# Metrics Architecture

## Overview

The payment platform uses **Prometheus** as the metrics backend and **Grafana** for metrics visualisation.

Two categories of metrics were collected:

1. **Application metrics** from the Spring Boot application.
2. **Infrastructure metrics** from the EKS worker nodes.

The overall architecture is:

```text
                   Application Metrics

Spring Boot Application
        |
        v
Micrometer
        |
        v
Spring Boot Actuator
        |
        v
/actuator/prometheus
        |
        | scrape
        v
     Prometheus
        |
        v
      Grafana


                Infrastructure Metrics

EKS Worker Node
        |
        v
Prometheus Node Exporter
        |
        | exposes node metrics
        v
     Prometheus
        |
        v
      Grafana
```

---

## Application Metrics

The Spring Boot application uses **Micrometer** to instrument application metrics.

Micrometer provides a vendor-neutral metrics instrumentation API for JVM applications.

The Prometheus registry exposes Micrometer metrics in a format that Prometheus can scrape.

Conceptually:

```text
Application activity
       |
       v
   Micrometer
       |
       v
Prometheus Registry
       |
       v
Spring Boot Actuator
       |
       v
/actuator/prometheus
```

Application metrics can include:

```text
HTTP request count
HTTP request duration
JVM memory usage
JVM garbage collection
Thread information
Process CPU usage
Application uptime
```

---

## Spring Boot Actuator

Spring Boot Actuator provides management and observability endpoints.

Application health was verified using:

```bash
curl http://localhost:8081/actuator/health
```

A healthy application returned:

```json
{
  "status": "UP"
}
```

The Prometheus metrics endpoint is exposed through:

```text
/actuator/prometheus
```

Unlike logs, Prometheus metrics use a **pull-based model**.

The application does not continuously push these metrics to Prometheus.

Instead:

```text
Prometheus
    |
    | HTTP GET periodically
    v
/actuator/prometheus
    |
    v
Metric samples
```

Prometheus performs this operation according to its configured scrape interval.

---

## Prometheus

Prometheus acts as the central time-series metrics backend.

Its responsibilities include:

```text
Discover targets
      |
      v
Scrape metrics
      |
      v
Store time-series data
      |
      v
Evaluate PromQL queries
      |
      v
Provide data to Grafana
```

In this environment, the Prometheus server was running as a **StatefulSet**.

This should not be confused with Node Exporter, which runs as a DaemonSet.

---

## Infrastructure Metrics with Node Exporter

Application metrics tell us what is happening **inside the application**.

Infrastructure metrics tell us what is happening on the **EKS worker nodes**.

Prometheus Node Exporter provides metrics such as:

```text
Node CPU utilisation
Memory usage
Filesystem usage
Disk statistics
Network statistics
System load
```

Node Exporter was enabled through the Prometheus Helm `values.yaml`.

It was not deployed using a manually written DaemonSet manifest.

Conceptually:

```text
Prometheus Helm values
        |
        | Node Exporter enabled
        v
Prometheus Helm Chart
        |
        | renders Kubernetes resources
        v
Node Exporter DaemonSet
        |
        v
One Node Exporter pod per EKS worker node
```

The architecture therefore becomes:

```text
Node 1 ── Node Exporter ──┐
                          |
Node 2 ── Node Exporter ──┼──> Prometheus
                          |         |
Node 3 ── Node Exporter ──┘         |
                                    v
                                  Grafana
```

Running Node Exporter as a DaemonSet ensures that each worker node can expose its own infrastructure metrics.

---

## Prometheus vs Node Exporter

These components perform different jobs.

### Node Exporter

Node Exporter:

```text
Collects operating-system/node metrics
            +
Exposes those metrics over HTTP
```

It does **not** act as the central metrics database.

### Prometheus

Prometheus:

```text
Discovers metric targets
        |
        v
Scrapes targets
        |
        v
Stores time-series data
        |
        v
Executes PromQL
        |
        v
Serves metrics to Grafana
```

Therefore:

```text
Node Exporter = metric producer

Prometheus = scraper + time-series database + query engine

Grafana = visualisation layer
```

---

# Latency Metrics

One important application metric monitored during the project was HTTP request latency.

Average latency alone can hide slow requests.

For this reason, percentile-based latency measurements such as **P95 and P99** are commonly used.

---

## P95 Latency

P95 represents the latency threshold below which approximately **95% of requests complete**.

For example:

```text
P95 = 200 ms
```

means approximately:

```text
95% of requests <= 200 ms

5% of requests > 200 ms
```

---

## P99 Latency

P99 focuses further into the slowest requests.

For example:

```text
P99 = 800 ms
```

means approximately:

```text
99% of requests <= 800 ms

1% of requests > 800 ms
```

P99 is particularly useful for identifying **tail latency** that may not be visible from averages.

---

## Prometheus Histograms

Latency percentiles can be calculated from Prometheus histogram metrics.

A typical histogram exposes buckets similar to:

```text
http_server_requests_seconds_bucket
```

Prometheus records how many requests fall within each latency boundary.

PromQL can then estimate a percentile using `histogram_quantile()`.

A representative P95 query is:

```promql
histogram_quantile(
  0.95,
  sum(
    rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket[5m])
  ) by (le)
)
```

For P99:

```promql
histogram_quantile(
  0.99,
  sum(
    rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket[5m])
  ) by (le)
)
```

The exact metric name and labels depend on the Micrometer and Spring Boot configuration being used.

---

# Why Metrics Alone Are Not Enough

Metrics are excellent for detecting that something is wrong.

For example:

```text
Grafana alert/dashboard

P99 latency
     |
     v
1.8 seconds
```

This tells us that some requests are slow.

However, the metric alone may not explain **where those 1.8 seconds were spent**.

That is where distributed tracing becomes useful.

For example:

```text
Prometheus:

P99 = 1.8 seconds

             ↓ investigate

Distributed Trace:

HTTP /payment               1.8 s
 |
 +-- Request validation     20 ms
 |
 +-- Database query        120 ms
 |
 +-- Downstream service    1.6 s
```

Prometheus helps answer:

> "Is the application slow?"

Distributed tracing can help answer:

> "Which operation or downstream dependency made this particular request slow?"

This is why metrics and traces complement each other.

---

# Metrics Pipeline Summary

The implemented metrics architecture can be summarised as:

```text
APPLICATION METRICS

Spring Boot
    |
    v
Micrometer
    |
    v
Actuator
    |
    v
/actuator/prometheus
    |
    | scrape
    v
Prometheus StatefulSet
    |
    v
Grafana


INFRASTRUCTURE METRICS

EKS Worker Nodes
    |
    v
Node Exporter DaemonSet
    |
    | scrape
    v
Prometheus StatefulSet
    |
    v
Grafana
```

---

## Key Lessons

The main lessons from the metrics implementation were:

**Micrometer generates/instruments application metrics.**

**Spring Boot Actuator exposes those metrics.**

**The Prometheus registry converts them into a Prometheus-compatible representation.**

**Prometheus periodically scrapes the metrics endpoint and stores the resulting time-series data.**

**Node Exporter exposes operating-system metrics from each EKS worker node.**

**The Prometheus Helm chart creates the Node Exporter DaemonSet when the component is enabled through Helm values.**

**Prometheus itself runs separately from Node Exporter and acts as the central metrics backend.**

**PromQL is used to query and aggregate the stored time-series data.**

**Grafana queries Prometheus and visualises the results.**

**P95 and P99 help expose tail latency that averages can hide.**